<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Provisional Government of the Republic of Korea</h1><p class="st-meta">1919 CE – 1948 CE · capital Shanghai, later Chongqing</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Korean government-in-exile founded in Shanghai after the March First Movement of 1919, which kept the cause of independence alive until 1945.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the wake of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement">March First Movement</a>, independence activists met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai-french-concession">French Concession</a> and in April 1919 proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">Republic of Korea</a> with a provisional constitution and assembly. Rival groups in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladivostok">Vladivostok</a> merged with it later that year, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee">Syngman Rhee</a> was chosen as president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The government drew funds from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> abroad and ran diplomacy and propaganda, but it was weakened by factional disputes; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee">Rhee</a> was impeached in 1925. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-gu">Kim Gu</a> it organised attacks on Japanese leaders, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yun-bong-gil">Yun Bong-gil's</a> bombing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1932, then moved through several Chinese cities before settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> in 1940, where it formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-liberation-army">Korean Liberation Army</a> and declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1941.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> never recognised it, and its leaders returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in late 1945 only as private individuals. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">Republic of Korea's</a> constitution claims descent from it, and it ceased to exist when that state was founded in 1948.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/provisional-government-of-the-republic-of-korea.jpg" alt="Provisional Government of the Republic of Korea" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Lumia1234 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Provisional_Government_of_the_Republic_of_Korea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1919 CE – 1948 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shanghai, later Chongqing</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Syngman Rhee, Kim Gu</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Korean</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China, South Korea</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Korean Provisional Government, KPG, Daehan Minguk Imsi Jeongbu</dd></dl></aside></div>

